THINK = {
1: r"Electricity is easier to understand if you picture water in pipes: voltage is the pressure, current is the flow, and resistance is how narrow the pipe is. The picture is not perfect, but it gives the right instincts. Before you touch a circuit, ask three questions: where does the current come from, where does it go, and what limits it?",
2: r"Ohm's law is a relationship between three quantities, so knowing any two gives the third. The habit worth forming is to write the formula with units and let the units check your work. A simulation is a cheap experiment: use it to test your prediction before you build.",
3: r"Every complicated network is built from two arrangements: parts sharing the same current (series) and parts sharing the same voltage (parallel). The two conservation laws say what flows into a junction flows out, and what rises around a loop falls back. If you can account for current and voltage like money in a ledger, any circuit becomes solvable.",
4: r"A capacitor and an inductor are about \emph{change}. A capacitor resists a sudden change of voltage, an inductor a sudden change of current, and both take time to respond. The time constant tells you how long. When a circuit does something surprising, ask what is storing energy and what is being asked to change quickly.",
5: r"A diode is a gate that opens only one way, and after it opens it holds the voltage almost constant. That single idea gives LEDs, rectifiers and protection circuits. Choose the simplest model that answers your question: an ideal switch for quick estimates, a fixed drop for design.",
6: r"A transistor is a valve: a small signal at the control terminal opens or closes a large path. Use it as a switch and you get digital logic; use it in between and you get amplification. CMOS pairs two complementary valves so that one is always closed, which is why idle chips use almost no power.",
7: r"An op-amp is a tool for making a circuit obey a simple rule. With feedback it adjusts its output until its two inputs are equal, so the gain depends only on resistors you choose. Think ``the op-amp will do whatever it takes to make the inputs match'' and most circuits become easy to read.",
8: r"A digital circuit throws away detail on purpose: only ``high'' and ``low'' matter, so noise cannot accumulate. Once everything is zeros and ones, design becomes logic, and logic can be written, simplified and proved like algebra. Always check a simplification twice, once with algebra and once with a truth table.",
9: r"Combinational design is like building with plumbing: signals flow through, and the output simply reflects the input after a delay. The delay matters: a long chain of gates is slow, which is why the structure of an adder affects how fast a computer can run.",
10: r"Memory comes from feeding an output back into the circuit that produced it. A clock turns this chaotic idea into an orderly one: everything updates together on a tick. To design a state machine, first draw the picture of states and arrows in words, then convert to logic, never the other way around.",
11: r"Hardware description is a different way of thinking from programming: everything you write exists at once and works in parallel. Ask of every line, ``what wires and gates is this?'' not ``what runs next?'' A testbench is your lab bench, so write it before you trust the design.",
12: r"Passing simulation does not mean the design is right, so build tests that check themselves. Compare against a golden model you trust, watch for unknown values, and treat every warning as a possible bug. The bugs here are classic, and seeing them once saves hours later.",
13: r"An FPGA flow turns text into wires through a chain of automatic steps, and each step can fail in its own way. Learn the chain so that when something goes wrong you know which stage to blame. Constraints are a promise to the tools about the real world; if the promise is wrong, the hardware will be too.",
14: r"An ALU is a box that computes every operation at once and then picks the one you asked for. Decide the contract first, including what each flag means, then write the code. Testing the flags as carefully as the result is where most ALU bugs are caught.",
15: r"A processor is surprisingly few parts: storage, a computing block, buses to move data, and a controller. The controller is the conductor, and the datapath is the orchestra. Separate the two in your mind, and the design stops looking like a tangle.",
16: r"A CPU is a loop: fetch an instruction, work out what it means, do it, repeat. The instruction set is the contract between the programmer and the machine, so freeze it before writing the hardware. Once your own CPU runs a program, you understand what every computer is doing underneath.",
17: r"A breadboard is a place where the circuit on paper meets the real world, with loose contacts and stray wires. Build neatly, keep wires short, and measure at each step so that when something fails you know where. Debug by changing one thing at a time.",
18: r"A circuit board is a schematic made permanent, so mistakes are costly. Work in a pipeline, check each stage before moving on, and think like the factory that will receive your files. Footprints and design rules are the contract between you and it.",
19: r"A microcontroller packs a small CPU, memory and input/output on one chip, so many problems that once needed a custom design now need only software. Knowing both tools lets you choose: an FPGA for parallel, timing-exact work, an MCU for flexible, cheap control.",
}
